# Programs that keep NVIDIA's own OpenGL when the session runs OpenGL on zink
# (sparky-stereo-opengl.sh): one program per line, the name of the executable its menu entry
# starts. CUDA and OpenGL sharing needs NVIDIA's own OpenGL: under zink CUDA finds no device
# behind the OpenGL context (cuGLGetDevices and cuGraphicsGLRegisterBuffer return
# CUDA_ERROR_INVALID_GRAPHICS_CONTEXT, measured on an RTX 3060 with driver 615.71.09; CUDA
# driver API, "OpenGL Interoperability": the CUDA devices are those of the current OpenGL
# context). /etc/sparky-stereo/opengl-programs and ~/.config/sparky-stereo/opengl-programs
# add a program ("NAME nvidia") or give one back to zink ("NAME zink").
#
# DaVinci Resolve (Blackmagic Design) shares its CUDA images with OpenGL for its viewers: when
# its OpenGL is not NVIDIA's, its log says "Failed to register OpenGL object for CUDA interop"
# and the viewer stays black (Resolve 20.3.2, github.com/niri-wm/niri/issues/3511, 2026-02-25).
resolve
# NVIDIA's own control panel: its OpenGL and GLX pages describe NVIDIA's GLX.
nvidia-settings
